Hmm. I'd rather that such a trove became a lot
_harder_ to find than just "you quaff a potion of
object detection".

This issue came up in discussions of how to remove
the predictability of the top level of Sokoban, and
perhaps other places in the game, that the shape of
the level tells you which loot to expect, letting
you decide to abandon the chase if it isn't what you
want.

[Can you say "Mines End"?]

It's worth repeating, though, since it got lost in
that discussion.

I propose a new kind of "one room" level,
specifically to be used to hide treasures, with a
new kind of access, a portal invisible to magic
orbs, in which special treasures can be situated.
The idea is to locate one of these where the
treasure would normally be located, say a Sokoban
closet, a treasure map "X" so that instantiation of
the treasure is separate and decoupled from layout
of the room.

Walking onto the portal (could be one use, but
doesn't need to be), would port the PC to the new
dungeon level (pets would not come along, there's no
place for them to sit), one grid space, containing
the return portal (activated, say, by the #sit
command, since there is no way provided to step off
and then on again, that lack of a second accessible
grid cell being designed to remove the chance for
random monster generation), which is a no teleport,
no levelport level, and that single grid cell plus
all the remaining rock undiggable. In that room
could be either the treasure, or a container holding
the treasure, as desired.

Of necessity, I think, this would be a "no bones
files allowed" level too, like main dungeon levels
1-3, quest top and bottom levels, the sanctum, and
such.

The goal here is, in the case of Sokoban, to make
the treasure still a mystery when the player sees
the layout of the top level, and in the case of
Ardvark Joe's treasure chests, to make the treasure
invisible to potions of object detection.

Other than that, the only implication for game play
would be that the new level would be a dandy place
to rest and recover hit points if the portal were
made multiple use, making that a game balance
decision to be considered against "but what if the
unspoiled player fails to grab the treasure the
first time?".

